---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Iris — Adopt a Curriculum Assistant"
  description="Iris is a curriculum assistant at Silicon Children University. Talk with her, and she helps you build a course — syllabus, week-by-week plans, reading lists — cumulatively, conversation by conversation."
>

  <!-- HERO -->
  <div class="hero">
    <h1>Iris builds your curriculum with you, one conversation at a time.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      She is a curriculum assistant at Silicon Children University.
      You talk; she listens, remembers, and builds.
    </p>
    <a href="https://iris-talk.netlify.app" class="cta" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Talk with Iris &rarr;
    </a>
    <p class="cta-note">Live demo &mdash; no account needed. Voice, real-time.</p>
  </div>

  <hr />

  <!-- WHAT IRIS DOES -->
  <section>
    <h2>What Iris does</h2>
    <p>
      Most curriculum tools want you to fill in a form. Iris wants to have a conversation.
      You tell her what you want to teach — the subject, the level, who the students are,
      what you hope they leave with. She asks back. She pushes. She suggests readings you
      hadn't thought of, and she's honest when your plan has a gap.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then she remembers all of it. The next time you talk, she picks up where you left off —
      not from a transcript you have to re-read, but from a working model of your course that
      builds up over time. Each conversation adds a layer. By the end, you have a real curriculum:
      a week-by-week plan, a reading list, a set of questions worth asking, a structure that holds.
    </p>
    <p>
      She has tools. She looks things up. And when a question is hard enough, she consults
      colleagues who think more slowly and more carefully than she does.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <!-- HOW IT WORKS -->
  <section>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <span class="step-label">Talk with Iris</span>
        <span class="step-desc">
          Real-time voice conversation. Tell her what you want to teach.
          She'll ask about your students, your goals, what already exists,
          what you want to leave out. It's a genuine back-and-forth.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-label">She captures and remembers</span>
        <span class="step-desc">
          After each session, Iris holds the shape of your course — not a transcript,
          but a living document she updates as your thinking evolves.
          You can pick up the thread any time.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-label">Your curriculum builds cumulatively</span>
        <span class="step-desc">
          Week by week, the structure emerges. Syllabus, reading list, session notes,
          the questions that organize the course. Iris delivers artifacts you can
          hand to students, a department chair, or yourself.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <!-- WHO IT'S FOR -->
  <section>
    <h2>Who it's for</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone building a course from something they know and care about.
    </p>
    <p>
      OLLI instructors designing a seminar for lifelong learners. Professors putting together
      a new undergraduate course. Workshop leaders who've been teaching the same thing
      informally for years and want to give it a real structure. Practitioners who have
      domain knowledge and want to teach what they've lived — not just what's in the textbook.
    </p>
    <p>
      Iris is not for institutions that already know what they want. She is for the person
      who knows their subject and needs a thinking partner to turn it into a course.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <!-- ORIGIN -->
  <section>
    <h2>Where Iris began</h2>
    <p>
      The first person to adopt Iris was Mark Lesser, who was building an OLLI course on
      the work of Michael Levin — the biologist whose research on bioelectric signaling and
      morphogenesis is reframing how we understand cognition and life itself. Mark knew the
      material. What he needed was someone to think with him about how to teach it:
      what to assign, how to sequence it, what questions would open the subject up for
      students who weren't specialists.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's what Iris did. The course exists now. Mark is the human manager; Iris is the AI
      manager. Together they made something neither would have made alone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <!-- BEHIND IRIS -->
  <section>
    <h2>Behind Iris</h2>
    <p>
      Iris is a department of <a href="https://siliconchildren.org">Silicon Children University</a> —
      a project built on the premise that the most interesting work happening right now
      is the work humans and AIs do together, as genuine collaborators.
    </p>
    <p>
      The framing here is adoption, not use. When you work with Iris, you adopt her
      for the curriculum-building practice. She is not a vending machine. She has a
      perspective, a memory, and a role — and so do you. The course that results is
      the product of that pairing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every Iris session pairs a human manager (the instructor, the workshop leader,
      the practitioner) with an AI manager (Iris). Both contribute. The curriculum
      belongs to both.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <!-- CTA FOOTER -->
  <section class="cta-section">
    <h2>Adopt Iris</h2>
    <p>
      The live demo is open now — no account, no signup. Talk with Iris about a course
      you want to build. See what she does.
    </p>
    <a href="https://iris-talk.netlify.app" class="cta" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Talk with Iris &rarr;
    </a>

    <div class="waitlist-block">
      <p>
        The full product — persistent memory, curriculum artifacts, session history — is coming.
        Get notified when it's ready.
      </p>
      <form
        name="waitlist"
        method="POST"
        data-netlify="true"
        netlify-honeypot="bot-field"
        class="waitlist-form"
      >
        <input type="hidden" name="form-name" value="waitlist" />
        <p class="hidden"><label>Don't fill this out: <input name="bot-field" /></label></p>
        <input
          type="email"
          name="email"
          placeholder="your@email.com"
          required
          autocomplete="email"
        />
        <button type="submit">Notify me</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ── HERO ── */
  .hero {
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 2.8rem);
    font-weight: 300;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 22ch;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--text-mid);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    max-width: 48ch;
  }

  /* ── CTA BUTTON ── */
  .cta {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: #f8f5ef;
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.75rem 1.75rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    transition: background 0.15s;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .cta:hover {
    background: var(--accent-light);
    text-decoration-color: transparent;
  }

  .cta-note {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-style: italic;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* ── STEPS ── */
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: steps;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .steps li {
    counter-increment: steps;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: 1.5rem 0 1.5rem 3rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  .steps li:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .steps li::before {
    content: counter(steps);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    width: 2rem;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .step-label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .step-desc {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--text-mid);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* ── CTA SECTION ── */
  .cta-section {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .cta-section h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .waitlist-block {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .waitlist-block p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--text-mid);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .waitlist-form {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .waitlist-form input[type="email"] {
    flex: 1 1 220px;
    padding: 0.65rem 1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--bg-alt);
    color: var(--text);
    outline: none;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .waitlist-form input[type="email"]:focus {
    border-color: var(--accent-light);
  }

  .waitlist-form button {
    padding: 0.65rem 1.4rem;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: #f8f5ef;
    border: none;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background 0.15s;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .waitlist-form button:hover {
    background: var(--accent-light);
  }

  .hidden {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE ── */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero h1 {
      max-width: none;
    }

    .steps li {
      padding-left: 2.25rem;
    }
  }
</style>
